When a spherical soap bubble lands on a horizontal wet surface and forms a hemisphere of the same volume, the radius of the hemisphere is equal to the radius of the original bubble multiplied by \sqrt{2}.

\begin{align}
    r_h = r_b \sqrt{2}
\end{align}

Given radius of hemisphere $r_h = 3\sqrt[3]{2}$ cm, we can solve for radius of the original bubble as follows:

\begin{align}
    r_b = \frac{r_h}{\sqrt{2}} &= \frac{3\sqrt[3]{2}}{\sqrt{2}} \\
    &= 3\left(\frac{\sqrt[3]{2}}{\sqrt{2}}\right) \\
    &= 3\left(\frac{\sqrt[3]{2}}{\sqrt[3]{2^2}}\right) \\
    &= 3\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt[3]{2}}\right) \\
    &= \frac{3}{\sqrt[3]{2}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt[3]{4}}{\sqrt[3]{4}} \\
    &= \frac{3\sqrt[3]{4}}{\sqrt[3]{8}} \\
    &= 3 \sqrt[3]{\frac{4}{8}} \\
    &= 3 \sqrt[3]{\frac{1}{2}} \\
    &= 3 \sqrt[3]{2}
\end{align}

So, the radius of the original bubble is $\fbox{3\sqrt[3]{2} cm}$. 
